{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"creamy-cocksure-elkhound","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Reproduction Steps\n\n1. Take epic `1386-5e7c-391d-4a74` (tearful-cleansable-gopher) and its children as authored.\n2. Run `rebar review-plan` on the epic and on children `35bc-aef6-efd5-4df5` and\n   `2aa6-726a-ea56-4d6c`.\n3. Observe every one returns **PASS with 0 blocking findings** and signs a certified\n   attestation. The epic's own signed manifest records `plan-review: PASS` / `blocking: 0` /\n   `advisory: 11`.\n4. Implement the tickets exactly as specified.\n5. Observe that **seven acceptance criteria across the three tickets cannot be satisfied**, and\n   that the contradictions are visible in the plan text itself — no implementation knowledge is\n   needed to see them.\n\n**Expected:** a plan whose acceptance criteria contradict each other, contradict their own\nScope, or contradict a sibling's acceptance criteria is not certifiable. Plan review should\neither block or, at minimum, raise these as findings.\n\n**Actual:** all three passed with zero blocking findings. Every contradiction surfaced later —\neither mid-implementation or at the completion-verifier close gate, after the work was done.\n\n## Why the plans were not completeable\n\nSeven instances, in four distinct failure classes. All quotes are the ORIGINAL text as\nreviewed; each has since been amended.\n\n### Class A — an AC contradicts its OWN ticket's Scope or another AC in the SAME ticket\n\nThe strongest class: both halves of the contradiction sit in a single document the reviewer\nread in full.\n\n1. **Epic `1386` AC1 vs its own Scope.** AC1: *\"No live source file under `src/rebar/` matches\n   `Success Criteria` / `success criteria` / `success criterion` (case-insensitive)\"*, granting\n   exclusions only for frozen fixtures and the vendor bundle. Its own **OUT OF SCOPE** section,\n   ~15 lines earlier, defers *\"the `dod_uncertifiable` / 'definition-of-done' vocabulary in the\n   verifier prompts\"* — three live files under `src/rebar/` that therefore keep matching\n   forever. AC1 could never pass.\n\n2. **Story `2aa6` AC1 vs its own Scope AND its own AC6.** AC1 required\n   `grep -rniE \"success[ _-]criteri(a|on)\" src/rebar/llm/reviewers/ ...` to return **no\n   matches**. Its Scope has a section headed **\"EXPLICITLY EXCLUDED\"** naming\n   `plan_review_T2.md:10`, and its **AC6** states that a grep on that same file *\"still returns\n   its one match\"*. AC1 and AC6 are directly contradictory: one demands zero matches in a\n   directory, the other demands one match in a file inside it.\n\n3. **Story `2aa6` AC3 vs AC6.** AC3 required the generated criteria guide's SC grep to be\n   *\"empty\"*. The guide is generated from the criteria registry and embeds each criterion's\n   prompt verbatim, so T2's AC6-protected phrase necessarily echoes into it.\n\n4. **Story `35bc` AC3 vs the epic's design.** AC3: *\"No test file under\n   `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/`, `tests/interfaces/store/` or\n   `tests/unit/test_project_portability_criterion.py` contains `## Success Criteria`.\"* But\n   sibling `5f4d`'s AC1 requires its reintroduction guard be *\"proven by a test that injects\n   one\"*. The epic therefore REQUIRES a file containing the literal string, and the natural home\n   is the contract test `35bc` itself adds. The completion verifier blocked the close on exactly\n   this, twice.\n\n### Class B — an AC contradicts a mechanical, checkable property of the artifact\n\n5. **Epic `1386` AC5.** *\"`docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` is REGENERATED (not hand-edited)\n   ... and contains no SC vocabulary.\"* The same criterion asserts the file is generated from\n   the registry, and the registry contains T2's excluded prompt. The criterion contradicts\n   itself in one sentence: regenerate from a source containing X, and contain no X.\n\n### Class C — an AC hard-codes a count that the plan elsewhere says to recompute\n\n6. **Epic `1386` AC4.** *\"The **108** store tickets carrying `## Success Criteria` are\n   migrated.\"* Child `0a8c`'s Scope explicitly says *\"the migration recomputes the set rather\n   than hard-coding it\"*. The real number at run time was **106**. A literal reading of AC4 can\n   never be satisfied, and a count in an AC is guaranteed to drift between authoring and\n   execution.\n\n### Class D — unowned scope: a parent AC that no child covers\n\n7. **Epic `1386` AC1 vs the decomposition.** AC1 requires clearing SC vocabulary from every live\n   file under `src/rebar/`, but `src/rebar/llm/completion.py:288` (*\"its success criteria are met\n   across children\"*) was covered by **no child story**: `35bc` took the detectors, `2aa6` the\n   reviewer prompts, `93d0` the eval specs, `44c7` the guides/docs. The union of children did not\n   cover the parent's AC, so the epic could not close however perfectly each child was executed.\n   This is notable because the criteria registry already contains **G3**, whose stated checklist\n   is *\"The union of children covers each parent acceptance/success criterion\"* — the exact\n   condition that was violated — and G3 routes at container scope. G3 ran and did not flag it.\n\n## Cost of late detection\n\nEach instance was found DURING implementation or at the close gate, not at review:\n\n- `35bc` AC3 — the completion verifier refused the close twice. Resolution required amending the\n  criterion, which invalidated the attestation as `stale-material` and forced a **full LLM\n  re-review** before the ticket could close.\n- `2aa6` AC1 and AC3 — found while implementing; same amend-plus-re-review cost.\n- Epic AC1/AC4/AC5 and the unowned `completion.py` site — found only by hand-auditing the epic's\n  AC against the real tree after three of six children were already closed and merged.\n\nSo the failure mode is not merely \"a bad plan shipped\": it is that **plan review certifies plans\nthat the completion verifier later refuses**, and the disagreement surfaces at the most expensive\npossible moment — after the code is written, reviewed, and in `35bc`'s and `0a8c`'s case already\nmerged. Every correction also costs a re-review, because amending a description invalidates the\nattestation.\n\n## Signal that the reviewer CAN catch a neighbouring class\n\nOn re-review, `2aa6` was correctly **BLOCKED** for a stale cross-ticket factual claim (its plan\nasserted prerequisite `1f32` *\"has NOT yet merged\"* when `show_ticket` showed it closed). So\ncross-ticket fact-checking against live ticket state does fire. The seven failures above are a\ndifferent class — internal consistency between an AC and its own Scope, its sibling ACs, or the\ndecomposition — and none of them fired.\n\n## Out of scope for this ticket\n\n**Do not** analyse or redesign the plan-review process here. No investigation of criteria\nprompts, routing, or detectors. This ticket exists to RECORD the evidence in detail; a follow-up\nsession owns the diagnosis and the fix.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] This ticket records all seven instances with the ORIGINAL criterion text, the specific\n      text it contradicts, and the ticket each belongs to — enough for a follow-up session to\n      reproduce the analysis without re-deriving it.\n- [ ] The four failure classes (A: intra-ticket contradiction; B: contradiction with a mechanical\n      property of a generated artifact; C: hard-coded count vs recompute-at-run-time; D: parent AC\n      not covered by any child) are each named with at least one worked example.\n- [ ] The evidence that these plans PASSED is recorded — epic `1386`'s signed manifest showing\n      `plan-review: PASS`, `blocking: 0`, `advisory: 11`.\n- [ ] The observation that G3 (\"the union of children covers each parent acceptance/success\n      criterion\") exists, routes at container scope, ran, and did not flag the class-D gap is\n      recorded as an observation only, with no diagnosis attached.\n- [ ] The late-detection cost is recorded: which gate actually caught each instance\n      (completion verifier / mid-implementation / manual audit) and that each fix forced a\n      re-review via `stale-material`.\n- [ ] No change is made to any plan-review prompt, criterion, routing entry or detector under\n      this ticket.\n","id":"e7be-b985-c344-46f7","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["plan-review","gate","process"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Plan review certified an epic whose acceptance criteria were mutually unsatisfiable"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785019852251663001,"uuid":"1b90d220-1a85-4c9a-bf82-53460bb6111d"}